1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Companies can call a request "received" while the customer still carries the work.
DueBack keeps the promise open until there is proof.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:28,000
This is the public English experience. The same complete flow is also available
in Spanish and Portuguese.

3
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:43,000
I choose the missing-refund example. The Spanish promise says Northstar Store
owes USD 59 for order ORDER-1842.

4
00:00:43,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Gemini 3.5 Flash analyzes the source asynchronously and builds a typed contract
with amount, reference, deadline, provenance, and uncertainty.

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Before any action, DueBack shows the exact channel, message, shared data,
limits, and proof requirement. The person approves this versioned scope.

6
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The first signed reply only acknowledges the request. DueBack rejects it:
acknowledgement is not proof, so the case remains open.

7
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Cloud Tasks retries in the background. Exact signed evidence then matches the
case, amount, currency, and reference. A deterministic verifier accepts it.

8
00:01:47,000 --> 00:02:08,000
This is the public architecture: Gemini through Genkit extracts without tools
or lifecycle authority. Policy, approval, action, and verification stay separate.

9
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Cloud Run hosts isolated services, Cloud Tasks provides bounded retries, and
Firestore preserves durable state. Hostile or mismatched evidence fails closed.

10
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:33,600
The counterparty shown here is an explicitly labeled controlled sandbox,
not a real merchant. DueBack: delegate the chase, keep the proof.
